"(PDF) Provincializing the Anthropocene (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/281845944_Provincializing_the_Anthropocene)\nciteturn10view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/281845944_Provincializing_the_Anthropocene\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1538\nL0: Article PDF Available\nL1: \nL2: # Provincializing the Anthropocene\nL3: \nL4:   * September 2015\nL5:   * cite0†Seminar A Journal of Germanic Studies 673:75-80\nL6: \nL7: Authors:\nL8: \nL9: cite1†  L10: \nL11: cite1†Kathleen D. Morrison L12: \nL13:   * cite2†University of Pennsylvania L14: \nL15: cite98†Image†www.researchgate.net L16: \nL17: cite3†Download full-text PDF cite4†Read full-text L18: \nL19: cite5†Download full-text PDF L20: \nL21: cite4†Read full-text L22: \nL23: cite6†Download citation L24: \nL25: Copy link Link copied\nL26: \nL27: * * *\nL28: \nL29: cite4† Read full-text cite6† Download citation L30: \nL31: Copy link Link copied\nL32: \nL33: cite7†Citations (34) cite8†References (13) L34: ## Abstract\nL35: \nL36: Essay published in Seminar, 2015. Is the Anthropocene a truly global concept, or is it a new form of extending Eurocentric histories? In this essay I consider the science of the Anthropocene from a methodological perspective, considering how preoccupations with specific time frames structures analysis. Like history, parts of the science of global change need to be 'provincialized.'\nL37: \nL38: cite99†Image: ResearchGate Logo L39: \nL40: Discover the world's research\nL41:   * 25+ million members\nL42:   * 160+ million publication pages\nL43:   * 2.3+ billion citations\nL44: \nL45: cite9†Join for free L46: \nL47: Public Full-text 1\nL48: \nL49: Content uploaded by cite1†Kathleen D. Morrison L50: \nL51: Author content\nL52: \nL53: All content in this area was uploaded by Kathleen D. Morrison on Sep 17, 2015\nL54: \nL55: Content may be subject to copyright.\nL56: \nL57: 75\nL58: \nL59: SEMINAR 673 – September 2015\nL60: \nL61: Pr o vincializing the anthr opocene\nL62: \nL63: KA THLEEN D. MORRISON\nL64: \nL65: THE suggestion that we have entered\nL66: \nL67: a new geological era, the ‘Anthro-\nL68: pocene’, an era in which humans for the\nL69: \nL70: first time must be counted as global\nL71: \nL72: agents, or drivers of change, cannot\nL73: \nL74: have escaped the attention of readers\nL75: \nL76: of Seminar. The assertion of a new\nL77: \nL78: form of agentive force for our species\nL79: \nL80: is subject to challenge in empirical\nL81: \nL82: terms, a point I discuss below. Evalu-\nL83: \nL84: ating the empirical sufficiency of the\nL85: \nL86: idea that significant human impact on\nL87: \nL88: the earth system is relatively recent is\nL89: \nL90: the subject of an ongoing research\nL91: project to collate and commensurate\nL92: \nL93: historical, archaeological, and paleo-\nL94: \nL95: environmental evidence regarding th e\nL96: \nL97: actual contours of the global human\nL98: \nL99: footprint (that is, a data-based rather\nL100: \nL101: than model-based reconstruction).\nL102: \nL103: While empirical sufficiency is impor-\nL104: \nL105: tant, the form that the Anthropocene\nL106: \nL107: debate takes is also of interest.\nL108: \nL109: In this essay, I discuss the some-\nL110: \nL111: what hidden Eurocentrism of the\nL112: \nL113: Anthropocene concept. T o a surprising\nL114: \nL115: extent, the notion of an Anthropocene\nL116: – and much of the analytical appara-\nL117: \nL118: tus surrounding it – represents an\nL119: \nL120: effort to expand (rather homogenized)\nL121: \nL122: European historical experiences, frame-\nL123: \nL124: works and chronologies onto the rest\nL125: \nL126: of the world. I take the term Eurocentr-\nL127: \nL128: ism here literally, in that existing mod-\nL129: \nL130: SEMINAR 673 – September 2015\nL131: \nL132: 76\nL133: \nL134: els tend to ‘build out’ from Europe and\nL135: \nL136: from the temperate zones, taking other\nL137: \nL138: regions as variants on an unmarked\nL139: \nL140: category. Building out from European\nL141: \nL142: history has given the Anthropocene\nL143: discourse a particular flavour, not only\nL144: \nL145: within the scientific community but\nL146: \nL147: also among those who have embraced\nL148: \nL149: the concept with the fervour of the con-\nL150: \nL151: verted, chiefly humanists for whom the\nL152: \nL153: idea of global anthropogenic agency is\nL154: \nL155: particularly new and exciting, and\nL156: \nL157: hard scientists who have finally man-\nL158: \nL159: aged to naturalize human social rela-\nL160: \nL161: tions into determinative models.\nL162: \nL163: I argue here that the concept of\nL164: \nL165: the Anthropocene is unnecessary – not\nL166: \nL167: because humans have not changed the\nL168: earth, but because we have done so\nL169: \nL170: throughout the Holocene. But even\nL171: \nL172: beyond this, it is important to note that\nL173: \nL174: the concept hides a disturbing exten-\nL175: \nL176: sion of colonial discourse into a post-\nL177: \nL178: colonial world.\nL179: \nL180: T\nL181: \nL182: he title of this essay is of course a\nL183: \nL184: homage to Dipesh Chakrabarty’s Pr o-\nL185: \nL186: vincializing Eur ope, which seeks to\nL187: \nL188: dislodge European thought from the\nL189: \nL190: centre of the practice of history, using\nL191: \nL192: the study of South Asia as a vehicle for\nL193: \nL194: so doing.\nL195: \nL196: 1\nL197: \nL198: Here I would suggest not\nL199: only that European historical experi-\nL200: \nL201: ences and the imagined relationships\nL202: \nL203: these imply about human population,\nL204: \nL205: land use, and human impact on the\nL206: \nL207: geosphere need to be decentred in\nL208: \nL209: analyses of anthropogenic environ-\nL210: \nL211: mental change, but further that the\nL212: \nL213: apparent novelty of a ‘geology of\nL214: \nL215: humans’ to both science and the\nL216: \nL217: humanities is just that – apparent.\nL218: \nL219: Provincializing the Anthropocene\nL220: \nL221: means not only that we no longer take\nL222: \nL223: European agricultural or industrial\nL224: history as a starting point, or that we\nL225: \nL226: stop trying to project (and retrodict)\nL227: \nL228: proposed causal relationships between\nL229: \nL230: population and anthropogenic effects\nL231: \nL232: derived from a limited sample of\nL233: \nL234: human economic history, but also that\nL235: \nL236: we attend to the ways in which existing\nL237: \nL238: ‘western’ structures of thought and dis-\nL239: \nL240: ciplinary practice overdetermine modes\nL241: \nL242: of agency – ‘human’ and ‘natural’.\nL243: \nL244: Thus it is that those disciplines\nL245: \nL246: most enthusiastic about declaring\nL247: \nL248: an end to the Holocene, already the\nL249: briefest geological period we know at\nL250: \nL251: ten thousand years, are those who, on\nL252: \nL253: the one hand, never before knew we\nL254: \nL255: were in it or, on the other, managed until\nL256: \nL257: now to analytically ignore or even\nL258: \nL259: erase human agency.\nL260: \nL261: A\nL262: \nL263: s parallel to Chakrabarty’s work,\nL264: \nL265: I offer here an alternative. Historical,\nL266: \nL267: paleoenvironnmental and archaeolo-\nL268: \nL269: gical research in India, among other\nL270: \nL271: places, shows us some of the limits of\nL272: \nL273: models and time markers built on an\nL274: \nL275: European base, challenging both the\nL276: form and substance of work which\nL277: \nL278: directly feeds in to global and local\nL279: \nL280: climate models and, as such, to sci-\nL281: \nL282: ence, policy, and disciplinary imagina-\nL283: \nL284: tions of the human place in the world.\nL285: \nL286: The poser to this issue contends\nL287: \nL288: that history matters for environmental\nL289: \nL290: issues in the present, an assertion\nL291: \nL292: true in at least two senses. First, it is\nL293: \nL294: empirically true. A rising tide of rese-\nL295: \nL296: arch is showing that humans have, in\nL297: \nL298: fact, been both biological and even\nL299: \nL300: geological agents for a very long time;\nL301: even the vast Amazonian rainforests\nL302: \nL303: once iconic of ‘pristine’ nature have\nL304: \nL305: been shown to be products of regrowth.\nL306: \nL307: 2\nL308: \nL309: This is a complex and variable history\nL310: \nL311: whose contours we must understand\nL312: \nL313: better, not only for their own sake, but\nL314: \nL315: for the present and the future.\nL316: \nL317: History matters, too, in how we\nL318: \nL319: generate and understand evidence\nL320: \nL321: about human-environment inter-\nL322: \nL323: actions. Those of us in fields long\nL324: \nL325: dedicated to understanding such\nL326: \nL327: engagements know just how difficult\nL328: it is to elude, for example, the funda-\nL329: \nL330: mental nature-culture dichotomy that\nL331: \nL332: so pervades both thought and lan-\nL333: \nL334: guage. The Anthropocene debate, for\nL335: \nL336: all its empirical redundancy and Euro-\nL337: \nL338: pean focus, may thus perhaps be in\nL339: \nL340: some ways a useful exercise after all.\nL341: \nL342: It has shown natural and physical\nL343: \nL344: scientists that humans can operate as\nL345: \nL346: more than simply ‘external’ distur-\nL347: \nL348: bance factors to ‘natural’ processes,\nL349: \nL350: and humanists that they, too, may have\nL351: \nL352: a role to play in addressing the current\nL353: environmental crisis.\nL354: \nL355: M\nL356: \nL357: ost proposals for an Anthropocene\nL358: \nL359: era adopt a rather limited historical\nL360: \nL361: perspective, assuming that significant\nL362: \nL363: environmental impact began only with\nL364: \nL365: the (European, and especially British)\nL366: \nL367: Industrial Revolution.\nL368: \nL369: 3\nL370: \nL371: This can\nL372: \nL373: become a self-fulfilling prophecy;\nL374: \nL375: consider the evidence on land trans-\nL376: \nL377: formation by humans reviewed in\nL378: \nL379: Ramankutty and Foley\nL380: \nL381: 4\nL382: \nL383: and Hook et\nL384: \nL385: al.\nL386: \nL387: 5\nL388: \nL389: which cover only the last 300\nL390: \nL391: years. While the significance of recent\nL392: anthropogenic change is beyond doubt,\nL393: \nL394: what is less clear is how novel such\nL395: \nL396: change really is. By shutting out con-\nL397: \nL398: sideration of longer-term change, we\nL399: \nL400: foreclose the possibility that anthropo-\nL401: \nL402: 1. D. Chakrabarty, Provincializing Eur ope.\nL403: \nL404: Princeton University Press, Princeton, 2000.\nL405: \nL406: 2. S. Hecht, K.D. Morrison and C. Padoch\nL407: \nL408: (eds.), The Social Lives of Forests: Past,\nL409: \nL410: Pr esent, and Futur e of W oodland Resur gence.\nL411: \nL412: University of Chicago Press, Chicago, 2014.\nL413: 3. Jan Zalasiewicz, Mark W illiams, Alan\nL414: \nL415: Smith, T iffany L. Barry, Angela L. Coe, Paul\nL416: \nL417: R. Bown, Patrick Brenchley, et al., ‘Are W e\nL418: \nL419: Now Living in the Anthropocene?’ GSA\nL420: \nL421: T oday, 18(2), 2008, pp. 4-8. See Kidwell,\nL422: \nL423: 2015, for an overview of the debate over\nL424: \nL425: timing within the scientific community.\nL426: \nL427: 4. Navin Ramankutty and Jonathan A. Foley,\nL428: \nL429: ‘Characterizing Patterns of Global Land Use:\nL430: \nL431: An Analysis of Global Croplands Data’,\nL432: \nL433: Global Biogeochemical Cycles 12(4), 1998,\nL434: \nL435: pp. 667-685.\nL436: 5. R. LeB. Hooke, J.F. Martin-Duque and\nL437: \nL438: J. Pedraza, ‘Land Transformation by Humans:\nL439: \nL440: A Review’, GSA T oday, 22(12), December\nL441: \nL442: 2012, pp. 4-10.\nL443: \nL444: 79\nL445: \nL446: SEMINAR 673 – September 2015\nL447: \nL448: genic change actually has a longer,\nL449: \nL450: more complex, or more variable trajec-\nL451: \nL452: tory than is generally assumed.\nL453: \nL454: This issue is actually critical to\nL455: \nL456: the debate, since there is ample evi-\nL457: \nL458: dence to suggest that even in the\nL459: \nL460: absence of farming, humans some-\nL461: \nL462: times drove vegetation change. For\nL463: \nL464: example, large-scale human burning\nL465: has reshaped vegetation regimes from\nL466: \nL467: grasslands to prairies which were once\nL468: \nL469: thought to be entirely ‘natural’. Agri-\nL470: \nL471: culture, of course, is another major\nL472: \nL473: means by which our species has\nL474: \nL475: reshaped not only vegetation, but also\nL476: \nL477: soils, slopes, hydrology, disease envi-\nL478: \nL479: ronments, the distribution of wild\nL480: \nL481: plants and animals and has made pos-\nL482: \nL483: sible new configurations of human\nL484: \nL485: population.\nL486: \nL487: I\nL488: \nL489: ndeed, it is the onset of agriculture\nL490: \nL491: that provides another magic number\nL492: in Anthropocene discourse, 6,000.\nL493: \n"
